// =============================================================================
// ATLAS Network — Tailscale access policy (REFERENCE COPY ONLY)
// =============================================================================
//
//   !!! THIS FILE DOES NOTHING ON ITS OWN. NOTHING READS IT AT RUNTIME. !!!
//
//   It is checked in only so the intended tailnet policy is reviewable in git.
//   To actually take effect it MUST be pasted, by hand, into:
//
//       Tailscale admin console  ->  Access Controls  ->  (edit the policy file)
//       https://login.tailscale.com/admin/acls/file
//
//   Paste it, press "Save", and fix up the operator identity below FIRST
//   (see "group:atlas-admins") or you will lock yourself out of your own
//   devices. Read that note in full: being in group:atlas-admins does NOT give
//   you admin reach from a PC that joined through the launcher, because the
//   tag on that PC replaces your user identity. Nothing in the launcher, the
//   network-gate Worker, or CI applies this file. Whenever you change it here,
//   re-paste it there.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THIS EXISTS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A brand-new tailnet ships with this default rule:
//
//     { "action": "accept", "src": ["*"], "dst": ["*:*"] }
//
// That means every ATLAS player's node can reach EVERY OTHER PLAYER'S ENTIRE PC
// on every port — SMB/file shares (445), RDP (3389), WinRM (5985), local dev
// servers, printers, everything. Players are anonymous strangers from Discord,
// not colleagues. The "acls" block below REPLACES that allow-all with the three
// rules ATLAS actually needs (two player-to-player port rules plus the operator
// escape hatch; ICMP needs no rule of its own — see below). Do not re-add the
// allow-all rule.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PORTS — every number below was read out of this repo, with the source cited.
// Nothing is opened on a guess.
//
// The line numbers were re-verified against the working tree the last time this
// file was touched, but main.dart and worker.js move constantly. The quoted
// symbol or literal on each line is the real anchor: if a number no longer
// lands on the quoted text, search for the text, don't assume the port changed.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TCP 3551  LawinServer / "ATLAS Backend" HTTP backend.
//           - atlas_link_flutter/assets/backend/lawinserver/index.js:28-29
//               const port = process.env.PORT || 3551;  -> express.listen(port)
//           - atlas_link_flutter/lib/main.dart:725
//               static const int defaultBackendPort = 3551;   (BackendProxyRouting)
//           - atlas_link_flutter/lib/main.dart:41288  remoteBackendPort: 3551
//               (the shipped LauncherSettings defaults)
//           Crosses the mesh: main.dart:16463 _useMeshPeerAsRemoteBackend()
//           points remoteBackendHost at peer.tailscaleIp (main.dart:16470) and
//           dials remoteBackendPort; main.dart:8893-8947
//           (_resolveBackendProxyTarget / _pingBackend) opens plain HTTP(S) to
//           that host:port over TCP.
//
// TCP 5595  Neonite V2 HTTP backend (the other embedded backend choice).
//           - atlas_link_flutter/assets/backend/neonite_v2/app.js:74-75
//               sails.lift({ port: 5595, ... })
//           - atlas_link_flutter/lib/main.dart:815
//               EmbeddedBackendType.neoniteV2.defaultPort => 5595
//           Same remote-backend path as 3551 — a player hosting Neonite hands
//           out <tailscale-ip>:5595.
//
// UDP 7777  Fortnite game server (the actual match traffic).
//           - atlas_link_flutter/lib/main.dart:1080
//               static const int _defaultGameServerPort = 7777;
//           - It is UDP, not TCP: main.dart:11254-11268 _pingGameServerPort()
//               uses RawDatagramSocket.bind(...) + socket.send(...) to probe it.
//           - assets/backend/lawinserver/Config/config.ini:21 (under the
//               [GameServer] section opened at :15)
//               port=7777      <- what the matchmaker hands to joining clients
//           - assets/backend/lawinserver/structure/matchmaking.js:40-41
//               "serverAddress": config.GameServer.ip,
//               "serverPort": Number(config.GameServer.port)
//
// ICMP      NO RULE IS NEEDED AND NONE IS WRITTEN BELOW. Tailscale does not
//           accept "icmp" as a "proto" value in an "acls" entry — the documented
//           list is igmp, ipv4/ip-in-ip, tcp, egp, igp, udp, gre, esp, ah, sctp,
//           plus raw IANA numbers 1-255. ICMP is handled out of band instead:
//           "If traffic is allowed for a given pair of IP addresses, then ICMP
//           will also be allowed."  (Syntax reference for the tailnet policy
//           file, https://tailscale.com/docs/reference/syntax/policy-file)
//           Because the two rules below already allow tag:atlas-mesh ->
//           tag:atlas-mesh traffic, `ping <peer-ip>` and `tailscale ping`
//           between player nodes already work. Do not add a "proto": "icmp"
//           rule to "acls" — it is undocumented syntax and may be rejected on
//           save. Do not "fix" that by dropping the proto field either: a rule
//           with no proto applies to ALL TCP and UDP traffic, which is exactly
//           the allow-all this file exists to remove.
//
// DELIBERATELY *NOT* OPENED (found while auditing, left closed on purpose):
//   TCP 80  — LawinServer's XMPP + matchmaker WebSocket
//             (assets/backend/lawinserver/structure/xmpp.js:8  const port = 80).
//             It is never contacted across the mesh: the URL handed to the game
//             is always loopback —
//               lawinserver/structure/matchmaking.js:18  "ws://127.0.0.1"
//               neonite_v2/api/controllers/MatchMakingController.js:57
//                                                        "ws://localhost:80"
//               lawinserver/CloudStorage/DefaultEngine.ini:4  ws://127.0.0.1
//             Opening 80 tailnet-wide would expose every player's local web
//             server for no benefit.
//   UDP 15009 — advertised as "BEACONPORT_i" in the fake session payload
//             (lawinserver/structure/matchmaking.js:59) but no code in this repo
//             connects to it, so it stays shut. IF (and only if) joins start
//             failing at the beacon step, add "tag:atlas-mesh:15009" to the UDP
//             rule below — do not add it pre-emptively.
//
// IF PLAYERS CHANGE THEIR PORTS: both are user-editable in launcher settings —
// the game server port (main.dart:8256 _effectiveGameServerPort, from
// _settings.hostPort) and the remote backend port (_settings.remoteBackendPort).
// A player who moves off the defaults will be blocked by this policy until the
// new port is added here. That is the intended trade-off; widen it explicitly.
//
// TAG NAME: "tag:atlas-mesh" is not arbitrary — it is exactly what the gate
// Worker stamps on every key it mints. The literal appears in FOUR places, and
// renaming it anywhere means renaming it in all four:
//   1. network-gate/wrangler.toml:41  TAILSCALE_TAG = "tag:atlas-mesh"
//   2. network-gate/src/worker.js:1081, inside mintTailscaleKey() (declared at
//        :1076) — the tag actually stamped onto each minted auth key:
//        const tag = (env.TAILSCALE_TAG || 'tag:atlas-mesh').trim();
//   3. network-gate/src/worker.js:1116, inside tailscaleDevices() (declared at
//        :1114) — the filter that decides which devices the admin panel can
//        see, kick and ban, and which ones the banned-IP sweep can remove.
//        SAME default string, second occurrence:
//        const tag = (env.TAILSCALE_TAG || 'tag:atlas-mesh').trim();
//   4. this file (tagOwners + every src/dst below).
// Miss #3 and the mint keeps working while the admin panel and the banned-IP
// sweep silently see zero devices — i.e. bans stop being enforced with no error
// anywhere. The wrangler.toml var overrides both worker.js defaults at once, so
// in practice you set it there and update this file — but if you edit the
// fallback literal in worker.js, edit BOTH copies.
// =============================================================================
{
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Who you are. EDIT THIS LINE before saving.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Replace the placeholder with the Tailscale login(s) of the operator(s) —
  // the same identity you sign in to the admin console with, exactly as it is
  // shown under Users (e.g. "you@github", "cipherzplayz@gmail.com"). Group
  // members must be real users of this tailnet; a placeholder will be rejected
  // on save, which is deliberate — it forces you to fill this in.
  //
  //   *** READ THIS — group membership does NOT follow you onto a tagged PC ***
  //
  // The last ACL rule gives group:atlas-admins access to everything, but that
  // rule matches a DEVICE only if the device still carries your user identity.
  // Any PC that joined the mesh through the ATLAS launcher does not: the gate
  // Worker mints a key tagged tag:atlas-mesh, and per Tailscale's docs
  // "Applying a tag to a device removes any user-based authentication" and
  // "The identity used for any Tailscale connection by a tagged device is the
  // set of tags assigned to the device."
  //   https://tailscale.com/docs/features/tags
  //   https://tailscale.com/docs/concepts/tailscale-identity
  // So from your own launcher-connected PC you are NOT an admin. You are a
  // player: 3551/5595 TCP and 7777 UDP to other players, nothing else. This
  // fails silently — no error, connections just hang or refuse.
  //
  // To actually have admin reach, pick one:
  //   (a) BEST — use a device that is NOT tagged. Install Tailscale on your
  //       admin machine and sign in normally (`tailscale up`, browser login,
  //       no key from the launcher). That device carries your user identity,
  //       so it matches group:atlas-admins and the last rule applies. Do not
  //       join that machine through the launcher; if you already did, open
  //       Admin console -> Machines -> that device -> ... -> Edit ACL tags and
  //       remove tag:atlas-mesh, then re-authenticate it as yourself.
  //   (b) Name the specific device instead. Uncomment the "hosts" block and the
  //       matching ACL rule at the bottom of "acls" and put that machine's
  //       100.x address in. src accepts a Tailscale IP or a host alias, and an
  //       IP matches regardless of tags. Caveat: launcher-joined devices are
  //       ephemeral (worker.js:1097, in mintTailscaleKey, sets ephemeral: true),
  //       so they are deleted on disconnect and the address is not guaranteed
  //       stable across one — only pin an address on a machine you joined
  //       yourself and intend to keep. Option (a) needs no maintenance;
  //       prefer it.
  // Either way, keep group:atlas-admins filled in and correct: it is what makes
  // option (a) work, and it is checked on save.
  //
  // NOTE: an ACL can never lock you out of the admin console itself, so a
  // mistake here is always fixable from https://login.tailscale.com/admin/acls.
  "groups": {
    "group:atlas-admins": [
      "REPLACE-ME@example.com",
    ],
  },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Optional: a name for one specific machine (option (b) above).
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // "hosts" maps a human-friendly name to a Tailscale IP or CIDR, and that name
  // is usable as an ACL "src". Matching by address is the only way to give admin
  // reach to a machine that carries tag:atlas-mesh, because the tag has replaced
  // its user identity. Uncomment this together with the last rule in "acls", and
  // use the device's real 100.x address from Admin console -> Machines.
  // "hosts": {
  //   "atlas-operator-pc": "100.101.102.103",
  // },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Who may attach tag:atlas-mesh to a device.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The gate Worker's Tailscale OAuth client mints auth keys carrying this tag,
  // so the tag MUST be listed here or every mint 4xx's and nobody can join.
  // An OAuth client is authorized by having the tag in its own scope
  // (Admin console -> Trust credentials -> edit the OAuth client -> devices:core
  // write, tag tag:atlas-mesh — see network-gate/wrangler.toml:56-59) plus this
  // entry.
  // "autogroup:admin" = any tailnet admin, which covers an OAuth client created
  // by an admin; group:atlas-admins is listed too so the named operator(s) can
  // tag devices by hand for testing.
  "tagOwners": {
    "tag:atlas-mesh": [
      "autogroup:admin",
      "group:atlas-admins",
    ],
  },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The actual access rules. THIS REPLACES THE DEFAULT ALLOW-ALL.
  // Anything not listed here is denied — that is the whole point.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "acls": [
    // Players -> players: HTTP backends only (LawinServer 3551, Neonite 5595).
    // Used when someone picks a peer as their remote backend.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["tag:atlas-mesh"],
      "proto": "tcp",
      "dst": [
        "tag:atlas-mesh:3551",
        "tag:atlas-mesh:5595",
      ],
    },

    // Players -> players: the game server itself. UDP only.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["tag:atlas-mesh"],
      "proto": "udp",
      "dst": ["tag:atlas-mesh:7777"],
    },

    // NO ICMP RULE HERE ON PURPOSE. ping / `tailscale ping` between player
    // nodes already works: Tailscale allows ICMP for any pair of IPs that is
    // allowed to exchange traffic at all, and the two rules above allow exactly
    // that. "proto": "icmp" is not documented for "acls" entries and may be
    // rejected on save; see the ICMP note in the header before adding one.

    // Operator escape hatch: full access to the whole tailnet, so you can debug
    // and run your own machines.
    // THIS ONLY MATCHES UNTAGGED DEVICES SIGNED IN AS A MEMBER OF THE GROUP.
    // A PC that joined through the ATLAS launcher carries tag:atlas-mesh, which
    // replaces its user identity, so this rule will NOT match it — see the long
    // note on group:atlas-admins at the top of this file for the two fixes.
    // To narrow this (recommended once things are stable), replace "*:*" with
    // the specific destinations you need, e.g. ["tag:atlas-mesh:*"]. To hand a
    // second person admin reach, add their login to group:atlas-admins above
    // rather than adding a rule here.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["group:atlas-admins"],
      "dst": ["*:*"],
    },

    // Option (b) from the group:atlas-admins note — admin reach from one named
    // machine, matched by address so a tag cannot shadow it. Uncomment BOTH
    // this rule and the "hosts" block above, and put the real 100.x address in.
    // Leave it commented out unless you are actually using it; an unused
    // allow-all-from-a-stale-address is a liability, not a safety net.
    // {
    //   "action": "accept",
    //   "src": ["atlas-operator-pc"],
    //   "dst": ["*:*"],
    // },
  ],

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Self-checks. The admin console runs these on save and refuses to save if a
  // rule stops matching. They are optional documentation-with-teeth — they
  // change no access on their own.
  // Syntax checked against the policy-file reference: a test entry takes src,
  // srcPostureAttrs, proto, accept and deny, and src accepts a tag exactly like
  // an ACL src does. So the tag-as-src and the proto field below are both
  // documented and safe to keep.
  // There is deliberately no ICMP test here. The docs' ICMP test example uses
  // "proto": "icmp" with port 0, but this policy grants ICMP implicitly (via
  // the TCP/UDP rules) rather than with a rule of its own, so a test asserting
  // it would be asserting behaviour this file does not itself express.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "tests": [
    {
      "src": "tag:atlas-mesh",
      "proto": "tcp",
      "accept": [
        "tag:atlas-mesh:3551",
        "tag:atlas-mesh:5595",
      ],
      // The whole reason this file exists: no lateral movement into a player's
      // SMB shares or Remote Desktop.
      "deny": [
        "tag:atlas-mesh:445",
        "tag:atlas-mesh:3389",
        "tag:atlas-mesh:5985",
      ],
    },
    {
      "src": "tag:atlas-mesh",
      "proto": "udp",
      "accept": ["tag:atlas-mesh:7777"],
    },
  ],
}
